---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/alternatives/rejoiner-alternative';
const title = "Rejoiner alternatives: separate software, services and creative handover";
const desc = "Choose what to retain before moving customer data, unfinished creative work and live journeys to a new team.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Must leaving the service mean leaving the software?",
    "answer": "No. Separate the platform, strategy and creative decisions using the applicable order and the work your team can operate."
  },
  {
    "question": "Do commissioned creatives automatically belong to the merchant?",
    "answer": "Confirm applicable rights, third-party licences and editable delivery formats in the agreement. Do not assume payment alone makes every asset portable."
  },
  {
    "question": "What must be accepted before enabling replacement journeys?",
    "answer": "Verify event identity, channel permission, purchase exits and remaining queue ownership with controlled fixtures, alongside approved creative assets and customer escalation coverage."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/alternatives">All alternatives</a>
    <p class="badge mt-6">Departure planning · November 25, 2026</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-6 text-xl leading-9">{desc}</p>
  </section>
  <article class="container prose-shopify pb-16">
    <h2>Do you need a different platform or a different service scope?</h2>
    <p>Rejoiner offers software alongside optional Strategy Partner and Creative Partner services on its <a class="link" href="https://www.rejoiner.com/pricing">current pricing page</a>. Guidance, production and platform access are separate purchasing decisions. Keeping the platform while changing the service brief can suit a team that now has its own operators. Keeping a production partner can suit a merchant whose bottleneck is approved creative work.</p>
    <p>Its <a class="link" href="https://www.rejoiner.com/products/text-messaging">text messaging page</a> also describes SMS capabilities; an email-only premise would misstate the choice. Verify which channels your order actually covers. The <a class="link" href="/apps/rejoiner">Rejoiner operating review</a> covers initial procurement; this departure plan concerns delivered assets, unfinished work and customer continuity.</p>
    <h2>Build an asset and obligation ledger</h2>
    <p>List live journeys, audience definitions, templates, source artwork, copy drafts, reports, domains, sender accounts and integrations. For each, record the account owner, format available, reuse permission and incoming custodian. Distinguish customer data from reusable supplier materials and third-party fonts or images. A rendered email is not necessarily editable source artwork.</p>
    <p>The <a class="link" href="https://www.rejoiner.com/terms">published terms</a> distinguish client data and licensed materials and tie services to an order. Review the applicable agreement and negotiated rights before treating commissioned work as portable. Request explicit delivery formats and reuse rights for assets needed in the replacement. Do not assume export access grants copyright ownership.</p>
    <h2>Close creative approvals before closing the engagement</h2>
    <p>Record every draft as awaiting facts, revision, approval, scheduled release or cancelled. Assign who can authorize sending; asset acceptance and campaign release are separate operational decisions. Reconcile any service acceptance provisions with the review process in your order. A late draft should not become a forgotten live campaign during the switch.</p>
    <p>Proposed acceptance fixture: hand the incoming team an approved product brief, the latest editable asset, a revision history and an unresolved inventory change. Ask them to produce a mobile preview and final destination links, then inspect who can release it. A second fixture uses a licensed image whose reuse is restricted: require a permitted replacement before release. These are proposed exercises, not observed supplier failures.</p>
    <h2>Translate the event contract, not just the template</h2>
    <p>For each journey write the entry event, customer identity key, required fields, eligibility, delay, offer and exit conditions. Record whether a Shopify purchase, cancellation or subscription change updates that journey and how delayed events are reconciled. Preserve channel-specific subscription evidence and exclusions; customer exports alone do not specify how a workflow behaves.</p>
    <p>Proposed fixtures: duplicate cart event, purchase before reminder, order from another device, missing product field and marketing opt-out while a step waits. Ask the replacement to show the event record and resulting queue action. Check email fallback content and SMS eligibility independently. If an event mapping fails, correct the mapping before comparing creative performance.</p>
    <h2>Choose a destination by the work it must own</h2>
    <p>For a team intending to operate lifecycle work internally, use <a class="link" href="/apps/klaviyo">Klaviyo</a>, <a class="link" href="/apps/omnisend">Omnisend</a>, <a class="link" href="/apps/drip">Drip</a> and <a class="link" href="/apps/sequenzy">Sequenzy</a> as review candidates against the actual email brief. Request demonstrations of event mapping, template portability and approval roles rather than assuming equivalent features. For dedicated SMS work, review <a class="link" href="/apps/postscript">Postscript</a> and <a class="link" href="/apps/attentive">Attentive</a> separately. No rank or automatic integration is implied.</p>
    <p>Retain useful campaign questions in the demonstration: does VIP access require a code, can waitlist priority follow available variants, should gift buyers receive a different story, and does a subscription renewal displace a promotion? Require the named operator to implement one approved example. Buying software leaves copywriting, QA, reporting and reply handling as work someone must still perform.</p>
    <h2>Compare capacity and contribution on the same basis</h2>
    <p>Obtain current quotes for active-contact software, channel usage, strategy, creative production, setup and overlap. Include merchant review hours and the incoming team's training and revision load. Compare approved work delivered and internal hours required alongside contribution; a lower platform bill alone does not establish a cheaper operating model. Review the <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing">cost worksheet</a>.</p>
    <p>Illustrative arithmetic: $120 order value less $12 discount and $48 product/fulfillment cost leaves $60 before marketing costs. If net sales already equal $108, subtract only $48 at that step. For a proposed holdout comparison, use contribution per assigned eligible customer over the same window and subtract service and transition costs once. Avoid dividing a whole retainer by one campaign and calling it the marginal cost of that send.</p>
    <h2>Hand over customers and remaining work together</h2>
    <p>Agree which system finishes already-queued steps and which accepts new events. Preserve a checkpoint, sent-message history and changes since export. During rollback, reconcile completed steps before re-enabling anything. Ask both providers about number continuity if SMS moves, and retain access to unresolved replies. Do not use a generic fixed transition period in place of these acceptance conditions.</p>
    <p>Assign a customer-facing escalation owner, backup approver and incident pause authority. Validate purchase exits, recipient-time restrictions and channel-specific permission under the <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">consent</a>, <a class="link" href="/guides/quiet-hours">quiet-hours</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">coordination</a> guides. Use <a class="link" href="/guides/campaign-restraint">campaign restraint</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/offer-discipline">offer discipline</a> for release rules. Close the handover only when asset rights, event fixtures, remaining approvals and customer queues have named custodians and recorded exceptions.</p>
    <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
